 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE DECLARATION OF ARBROATH. In 1320 the barons and nobles of Scotland sent a letter from Arbroath to the Pope, saying that as long as a hundred of them lived they would never submit to English rule: 'for it is not for glory, nor riches, nor honours that we are fighting, but for freedom'. The abbey itself had been founded in 1178 by William the Lion. It has kept the roof off ever since, on a technicality.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_arbroath",
   "title": "The Declaration of Arbroath"
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE BELL ROCK LIGHTHOUSE. Built on a reef eleven miles out to sea, off Arbroath, between 1807 and 1810 to the design of Robert Stevenson, and the oldest sea-washed lighthouse still standing in the world. The masons worked on the reef at low tide with the sea coming in round their ankles, and then went home for tea.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_bell_rock",
   "title": "The Bell Rock"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "DUNNOTTAR CASTLE. A ruined fortress on a headland south of Stonehaven. In 1651, with Cromwell's army closing in, the Honours of Scotland, the crown, sceptre and sword of state, were smuggled out past the besiegers and hidden in the church at Kinneff. They came back to Edinburgh after the Restoration and are in the Castle now.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_dunnottar",
   "title": "Dunnottar Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "ARBROATH SMOKIES. Haddock, salted and smoked in pairs over a hardwood fire in a half barrel, a method from the fishing village of Auchmithie. The name is protected, so a smokie from anywhere else is not allowed to call itself one."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "FOWLSHEUGH. Red sandstone cliffs south of Stonehaven that hold one of the great seabird colonies of the east coast: guillemots, razorbills and kittiwakes on every ledge, and the odd puffin, looking as if it has come to the wrong party."
  }
 ],
